# NetHub's runtime dependencies, the ones it imports directly. This file is
# the input; requirements.txt is the lockfile generated from it, with every
# transitive dependency pinned and hashed. Edit this file, then regenerate:
#
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --allow-unsafe --strip-extras \
#       --output-file=requirements.txt requirements.in
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --allow-unsafe --strip-extras \
#       --output-file=requirements-dev.txt requirements-dev.in
#
# on Python 3.12, the version the image and CI use: a marker that differs by
# Python version would otherwise resolve differently. CI regenerates both and
# fails on any difference, so an edit here without a regenerated lock does not
# merge. Everything installs with --require-hashes (Containerfile, CI).
#
# The direct pins stay exact rather than ranges, because CLAUDE.md makes
# load-bearing claims about specific versions ("the pinned gunicorn version
# (26.x) parses its app argument as a Python expression", and
# control_socket_disable existing at all); gunicorn has already changed
# relevant behaviour once at 25.1. A version bump is a deliberate diff here.
#
# PyYAML was dropped at build step 7 with the YAML registry it served. yamllint,
# ruff and zizmor are CI tools installed by the workflow, not dependencies.
Flask==3.1.3
Flask-SQLAlchemy==3.1.1
Flask-Login==0.6.3
Flask-WTF==1.3.0
# Schema migrations (PLAN.md WS-6; nethub/schema.py, nethub/migrations/).
# alembic is pinned in its own right, not left to Flask-Migrate's range: it is
# what actually rewrites the database at every upgrade.
Flask-Migrate==4.1.0
alembic==1.20.0
gunicorn==26.2.0
# Seals device credentials into the job row (PLAN.md WS-7). Already installed
# as a paramiko dependency; pinned in its own right because NetHub now calls
# it directly and a change in it changes what can open a stored credential.
PyNaCl==1.6.2
